	funcAddr(resultId ValueId, funcName ByteArray) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.memory, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: true, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: false, isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, clobbersFlags: false, isCmp: false}
	// A call THROUGH a function VALUE — `resultId = calleeReg(args…)`. `calleeReg` is the SSA value holding
	// the code pointer (from a `funcAddr`, a param, or a field load), NOT a name — the whole reason it is
	// distinct from `call`. Metadata identical to `call`: full call-barrier semantics. The x64/arm64
	// lowering moves each arg into its ABI register, calls THROUGH the callee register (`callReg` /
	// `arm64BranchLinkReg`), then moves the return value into `resultId`.
	//
	// ⭐ IT CARRIES THE FUNCTION VALUE'S SIGNATURE — `resultType` (the result's return type) and
	// `argFloatMask` (bit k set iff argument k is a float) — because, unlike a direct `call`, there is NO
	// callee NAME to recover it from (the name index / `calleeParamClasses` are keyed by name).
	// Without it every backend assumed an INTEGER result and integer args, so a float-returning or
	// float-taking function value colored a move across register files (P1.5 #78). The FRONT END already
	// types the result value and every arg value, so `lowerIndirectCall` reads both straight off `stdTypeOf`
	// — no parser or Maxon-op change. A backend routes arg `k` to a float or GPR register by bit `k` of the
	// mask (`args`'s trailing entry, the uniform-ABI env pointer, is never float) and captures the result by
	// `resultType`, exactly as a direct call reads the callee's declared types.
	//
	// ⭐ `resultType` IS A `StdReturnType`, NOT A BARE `StdType` — the union `void | value(StdType)` — because
	// an indirect callee can be genuinely VOID with NO result at all: the per-element destructor
	// (`__str_decref`/`__destruct_<T>`/`__mm_decref`/`__managed_decref`, a `(param i64)` void function) the array
	// drop walk invokes (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitElementDestroyRange`). A void function VALUE (`let cb = doIt; cb()`)
	// is DIFFERENT and stays `value(i64)`: it routes through a `__fnref_` thunk that returns a dummy i64, so
	// its `call_indirect` functype `(…)->i64` matches the thunk (LowerMaxonToStd.indirectResultType). The raw
	// void destructor has no such thunk — its true type is `(param i64)` with no result — so on `void` the
	// wasm functype interns ZERO results and the native backends capture no result register. Making the whole
	// wasm signature (not just the width) exact is what stops the `call_indirect` type check from trapping.
	//
	// ⭐ **THERE WAS A SIBLING WIDTH MASK AND IT IS GONE (WASM3).** `argNarrowMask` declared argument k
	// narrower than the machine word when its DECLARED type was an `i1`/`u32`. That is exact only while both
	// ends of the call read the same declaration, and a SHARED GENERIC BODY does not — see
	// `StdToWasm.indirectCallArgValType` for the measured trap. An indirect call's arguments travel at the
	// MACHINE WORD on every target now, which is what the register targets always did.
	//
	// ⚠ `resultType`/`argFloatMask` ARE SCALARS, DELIBERATELY. A per-arg `StdTypeArray` here
	// would be a SECOND
	// managed field on the variant, which the bootstrap that compiles shv2 mishandles in the union's
	// synthesized destructor — a wild free surfacing as an unrelated `mm_decref` underflow at teardown, on
	// EVERY indirect call. `StdReturnType` is a VALUE union (its `value` arm wraps the enum `StdType`, no
	// heap), so it adds no managed field: the mask and it keep `callIndirect` at ONE managed field
	// (`args`) exactly like its original shape — the same allocation-flat idiom `CalleeParamClassMap` uses to
	// answer the identical question for a call by NAME. `args` stays LAST (the one managed field),
	// scalars first. Both are METADATA, not value operands: `collectStdOpUses`/`remapStdOpUses` leave them
	// untouched.
	callIndirect(resultId ValueId, calleeReg ValueId, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, args ValueIdArray) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.call, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: false, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
	// A SOFTWARE PANIC: abort with the interned message at `.rdata` label `label`, printing it plus a
	// symbolized backtrace and exiting `PanicExitCode`.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO PRODUCERS, ONE OP, AND THE NAME SAYS SO.** It was `osRangePanic` and served only the P1.9
	// ranged-typealias guard (`InsertRangeChecks`); a user `panic("…")` reaches it too now
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerPanicAbort`), and the contract never was range-specific — it takes a baked
	// message and ends the process, which is all either caller wants. The op did not change; only the name
	// that had stopped describing it did.
	//
	// A `system`-band op — the same
	// shape and rationale as `osExit`/`osWriteStdout`: it says WHAT is wanted (panic with this message)
