	// `X64Runtime.emitPrintString` against `StringRuntime.buildPrintString`: two unrelated mechanisms one
	// letter apart.)
	osWriteStderr(resultId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
	// --- STACK PROMOTION: the address of a record this frame owns outright ---
	//
	// ⚠ APPENDED AT THE VERY END OF THE UNION, past `osWriteStderr`, per the band-append invariant.
	// Its `category` is `memory` (it computes an address, exactly as `globalAddr` does) and that
	// placement is independent of this tail position — the invariant is about compile-time
	// exhaustiveness, and the END is the only position at which EVERY range arm in the compiler
	// fails to compile rather than silently swallowing a new variant.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE RESULT IS `[frame slot region] + slot*8`, AND THE FRAME IS THE POINT.** `promoteStackRecords`
	// emits this in place of the `call __mm_alloc(size, 0)` a record constructor would have made, once it has
	// PROVEN the record cannot outlive the frame (see that pass's header for the proof obligation). The record
	// then has no MM header, no refcount, no `__mm_alloc_count` entry and no drop — which is why the pass must
	// delete the matching `__mm_decref` in the same edit, and why a wrong promotion is a dangling pointer
	// rather than a slow program.
	//
	// It names a SLOT, not a displacement, for the reason `TargetOp.storeSlotReg`/`arm64StoreOutgoingArg`
	// name one: the address is `spillSlotBaseDisp + slot*8` on x64 and `x29 + 16 + slot*8` on arm64, and
	// neither base is known until the register allocator has settled the frame. The slot indices `[0, k)` are
	// RESERVED out of the spiller's range (`SplitDriver.create` seeds `nextSlot` from `func.stackSize`), so a
	// promoted record and a spilled value can never be given the same slot.
	//
	// `isPure: true` — it recomputes an address that cannot change, so it is freely rematerializable, exactly
	// like `globalAddr`. `isMemory: false` — it computes an address; it does not touch memory.
	//
	// ⚠ `isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true` is the RIGHT value and it is enforced by NOTHING: the slot it names
	// belongs to THIS function's frame, so splicing it into another body would silently address a different
	// one — but that field has no reader anywhere in the compiler (see `StdOpMeta.isPure`'s note and OPEN.md
	// #27), so it is a declaration for whoever writes the first splice, not a guard that exists today.
	//
	// ⛔ **wasm32-wasi CANNOT RECEIVE THIS OP, and the gate is in the pass, not here.** A wasm local has no
	// address and the module emits no `__stack_pointer` global, so there is no frame for a slot to index
	// (`StdToWasm`'s header: *"the call stack is the ENGINE's, not addressable memory"*).
	// `promoteStackRecords` therefore does nothing on that target, and `StdToWasm`'s arms panic rather than
	// guess.
	stackRecordAddr(resultId ValueId, slot SpillSlot) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.memory, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: true, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: false, isStore: false, isCall: false, clobbersFlags: false, isCmp: false}
	// --- The DebugStream PRODUCER's three host primitives (Workstream O3) ---
	//
	// ⭐ **APPENDED AT THE VERY END OF THE UNION, per the band-append invariant** `witnessTryCall` states above:
	// every stale full-tail range arm (`… to stackRecordAddr`) fails to compile until it is consciously
	// extended, which is the mechanism that finds every site obliged to classify or lower a new op. Their
	// `category: system` places them logically in the OS band, independent of this tail position.
	//
	// ⭐ **THEY EXIST BECAUSE THE PRODUCER IS EMITTED CODE AND THE CONSUMER IS ANOTHER PROCESS.** `maxon
	// monitor` creates a named shared segment, hands its NAME to the target through the `MAXON_DEBUGSTREAM`
	// environment variable, and decodes the ring the target writes into it. Everything above that substrate —
	// the segment header, the schema handshake, the ring cursors, the entry packing — is builder-built Std in
	// `DebugStreamRuntime.maxon`, exactly as the FILETIME arithmetic above stays out of `osReadWallClock`.
	// These three are the irreducible host calls underneath it.
	//
	// ⚠ **ALL THREE ARE x64-WINDOWS ONLY, AND THE GATE IS THE DRIVER'S** (`--debugstream` is refused on any
	// other target, naming the gap). The panic arms in the arm64 and wasm lowerings are the backstop for a
	// compiler bug that got one past that refusal, never the user-facing rejection — `osReadWallClock`'s
	// arrangement exactly.

	// `osOpenNamedSegment(result, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) -> HANDLE (0 when there is no segment to open)`.
	// `GetEnvironmentVariableA(namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen)` then `OpenFileMappingA(FILE_MAP_ALL_ACCESS, FALSE,
	// bufPtr)` — the env lookup and the open are ONE op because the buffer between them is pure scratch that
	// no Std value ever needs to read, and splitting them would put a `.data`-addressed C string into the IR
	// for no consumer.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS BRANCH-FREE, AND THAT IS WHY THE ENV VAR BEING UNSET NEEDS NO ARM.** `__slab_alloc` hands
	// back ZEROED memory (the allocator's zeroing contract), so an absent variable leaves `bufPtr` naming the
	// EMPTY string — and `OpenFileMappingA("")` fails and returns NULL, which is the same answer "no monitor
	// is listening" gets by every other route. One value out, one test at the caller.
	osOpenNamedSegment(resultId ValueId, namePtrId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}

	// `osMapNamedSegment(result, handle, size) -> base address (0 on failure)`. `MapViewOfFile(handle,
	// FILE_MAP_ALL_ACCESS, 0, 0, size)`. A NULL handle maps nothing and returns NULL, so the failed-open case
	// above flows through here untested for the same reason it flowed into it — see `osOpenNamedSegment`.
	//
	// ⚠ `size` is the WHOLE segment length and it is the CONSUMER's number, not this producer's: the monitor
	// creates the section that long, and a `MapViewOfFile` longer than the section returns NULL. One
	// definition (`DebugStreamRuntime.DsSharedMemoryBytes`) is what keeps a silent "the trace never appeared"
	// from being the failure mode of a disagreement.
	osMapNamedSegment(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, sizeId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}

	// `osTickCountMs(result) -> milliseconds since boot`. `GetTickCount64()`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SAME COUNTER THE CONSUMER SEEDS THE SEGMENT WITH**, which is the whole of why it is this
	// primitive and not `osReadClock`'s QPC: the monitor writes `Environment.TickCount64` — GetTickCount64 —
	// into the header's `start_timestamp`, and every entry carries `now - start` as a millisecond delta. Two
	// different clocks either end would make that subtraction meaningless, and nothing would report it.
	osTickCountMs(resultId ValueId) = StdOpMeta{category: StdOpCategory.system, role: OpRole.plain, isPure: false, isUnsupportedInInlineBody: true, isMemory: true, isStore: true, isCall: true, clobbersFlags: true, isCmp: false}
end 'StdOp'

export typealias StdOpArray = Array with StdOp

// The set of value ids a function's ops READ. Every Std-tier pass that asks "does anything
// still read this value?" — the const DCE, the dead-block-arg prune, the trivial-block-arg
// elimination — reads it from `collectStdOpUses` below rather than re-matching the union, so a
// new StdOp variant is a compile error in ONE place instead of a silently-missed use at each of
// them. (This is the Std tier's counterpart to `targetOpOperands`, which plays the same role for
// the Target tier's liveness, splitter, colorer and checker.)
//
// A DENSE COLUMN, not a `Set`. A ValueId IS a dense, function-local index — so a hash set spends a
// hash and a probe to look up something that is already an array subscript. That is the design's
// "NO `Set`/`Map` and NO hashing in the hot path" rule (ARCHITECTURE.md §"Data representation")
// broken in the one place it is easiest to break it, and on the corpus's single large function it
// showed: `foldConstOperands` grew at exponent **1.29 in TIME while its ALLOCATIONS grew at 0.99** —
// the signature of a structure whose asymptotics are fine and whose CONSTANT degrades, because every
// probe into a table that doubles with the program is a cache miss that the previous rung did not
// take. It was invisible while the register allocator's quadratic dwarfed it at 87% of the compile;
// removing that made this the phase over its budget.
//
// It needs no size up front — the column grows to fit the highest id it is given — so it is a drop-in
// for the `Set` it replaces, with the same three operations and no hashing at all.
export type StdValueUseSet
	var member as BoolArray

	export static function create() returns StdValueUseSet
		return Self{member: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	export function insert(valueId ValueId)
		// `growFilled`, NOT `resize`: this column is extended ONE ENTRY AT A TIME (inserting ids in
		// increasing order is exactly what the collect walk does), and `resize` reserves EXACTLY the length
		// it is given — Θ(values²) of allocator traffic for a column that ends up Θ(values) long. Measured,
		// on the first cut of this very type: +4 GB at scale rung 5, with the byte curve at exponent 2.0.
		// `growFilled` grows the capacity geometrically and clears what the growth added.
		self.member.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: false)

		try self.member.set(valueId, value: true) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.insert: member.set OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'insert'

	export function contains(valueId ValueId) returns bool
		if valueId >= self.member.count() 'neverInserted'
			return false
		end 'neverInserted'
		return try self.member.get(valueId) otherwise panic("StdValueUseSet.contains: member.get OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'contains'
end 'StdValueUseSet'

// Add the value ids `op` READS to `referenced` — its DEFS are not uses, and neither are the
// branch-edge args (those live on the predecessor's `IrBlock`, not on the op). Exhaustive
// over StdOp, no bare `default`.
//
// EXPORTED, not module-scoped: the Std→x64 lowering reads it too, to decide which compare
// results are read as VALUES (and so need a `setcc`) rather than only as branch FLAGS. That
// is exactly the question this function exists to answer in one place — a second, private
// "what does this op read?" match inside the backend is the drift it was written to prevent.
export function collectStdOpUses(op StdOp, referenced StdValueUseSet)
	match op 'u'
		const then break 'u'
		binOp(_, lhs, rhs, _, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		unaryOp(_, operand, _, _) then referenced.insert(operand)
		cmp(_, lhs, rhs, _, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		binOpImm(_, lhs, _, _, _) then referenced.insert(lhs)
		cmpImm(_, lhs, _, _, _) then referenced.insert(lhs)
		div(_, lhs, rhs, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		mod(_, lhs, rhs, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: lhs, b: rhs)
		condBranch(condId, _, _) then referenced.insert(condId)
		branch then break 'u'
		ret(retValId) then referenced.insert(retValId)
		param then break 'u'
		call(_, _, args) then insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
		retVoid then break 'u'
		// `globalAddr` reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME, not a value, so it has no
		// operand at all (its result is a def). The other two read the address they are
		// handed, and a store reads the value it writes as well.
		globalAddr then break 'u'
		loadIndirect(_, addrId, _, _) then referenced.insert(addrId)
		storeIndirect(addrId, _, valueId, _) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: addrId, b: valueId)
		// The size is a real value operand — the only one this op reads; its result is a def.
		osAllocPages(_, sizeId) then referenced.insert(sizeId)
		// Both the buffer and the length are real value operands; the byte count is a def.
		osWriteStdout(_, bufId, lenId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufId, b: lenId)
		// `rdataAddr` reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME, not a value (its result is a def), exactly
		// like `globalAddr`.
		rdataAddr then break 'u'
		// A throwing call reads its arguments exactly as `call` does; `resultId`/`errorFlagId` are DEFS.
		tryCall(_, _, _, args) then insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
		// The dual-register terminator reads BOTH the primary value and the error flag it writes out.
		errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: retValId, b: errorFlagId)
		// The exit code is the one value operand `osExit` reads.
		osExit(codeId) then referenced.insert(codeId)
		// `funcAddr` reads nothing — its `funcName` is a NAME, not a value (its result is a def), exactly
		// like `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`.
		funcAddr then break 'u'
		// An indirect call reads the callee register AND its arguments; `resultId` is a def, and
		// `resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata (not value operands).
		callIndirect(_, calleeReg, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: calleeReg, args: args)
		// A range panic reads nothing — its `label` is a NAME (an `.rdata` symbol), not a value, and it
		// defines nothing.
		osPanic then break 'u'
		// Both out-param ADDRESSES are real value operands (the builder's `globalAddr` results); this op
		// defines nothing. The `ms` count is the one value `osSleepMs` reads. Both must be reported here or
		// the allocator would free the address/count registers early and the OS call would read garbage.
		osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: ticksPtrId, b: freqPtrId)
		osSleepMs(msId) then referenced.insert(msId)
		// P1.5 subprocess: each reads its operands and (except `osWaitHandle`) defines nothing. `osWaitHandle`'s
		// `resultId` is a DEF, so it is skipped here exactly as `osAllocPages`'s result id is.
		osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtr, startupPtr, procInfoPtr) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: cmdlinePtr, b: startupPtr, c: procInfoPtr)
		osProcessExitCode(handle, codePtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: codePtr)
		osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtr, count, timeoutMs) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: handleArrayPtr, b: count, c: timeoutMs)
		osCloseHandle(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osWaitHandle(_, handle, timeoutMs) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: timeoutMs)
		// The region base is the one value operand `osFreePages` reads; it defines nothing (like `osExit`/`osCloseHandle`).
		osFreePages(addr) then referenced.insert(addr)
		// The assembled message's address is the one value operand a rendered panic reads; it defines
		// nothing (the label-carrying `osPanic` reads nothing at all).
		osPanicValue(messagePtr) then referenced.insert(messagePtr)
		// No operands: `GetCommandLineA` reads the PEB.
		osCommandLine then break 'u'
		// The jump table reads ONLY the biased index `subjectId`; `denseTargetBlockIds`/`defaultBlockId` are
		// block ids (the CFG, carried on the op), NOT value operands, and are deliberately not bound here (a
		// tail-slot Array binding in this big match is the decref-corruption trap `switch` documents).
		switch(subjectId, _, _) then referenced.insert(subjectId)
		// A witness call reads the WITNESS-TABLE ADDRESS and its arguments — the exact shape of `callIndirect`
		// (`witnessAddr` in place of the callee register), so it reports uses the same way. `resultId` is a def;
		// `methodIndex`/`resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata (not value operands).
		witnessCall(_, witnessAddr, _, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: witnessAddr, args: args)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: each IOCP op reports its VALUE operands; its `resultId` (where present) is a DEF,
		// skipped here exactly as `osWaitHandle`'s is. `osIocpCreate`/`osEventCreate`/`osLastError` read nothing (a
		// pure result def, like `osAllocPages`'s but with no size operand).
		osPipeCreate(namePtr, saPtr, iocp, readPtr, writePtr) then insertUseQuint(referenced, a: namePtr, b: saPtr, c: iocp, d: readPtr, e: writePtr)
		osIocpCreate then break 'u'
		osEventCreate then break 'u'
		osThreadCreate(_, entryPtr) then referenced.insert(entryPtr)
		osLockInit(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osLockEnter(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osLockLeave(lockPtr) then referenced.insert(lockPtr)
		osReadOverlapped(_, handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl) then insertUseQuint(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: cap, d: bytesOutPtr, e: ovl)
		osLastError then break 'u'
		osProcessSpawnInherit(cmdlinePtr, startupPtr, procInfoPtr) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: cmdlinePtr, b: startupPtr, c: procInfoPtr)
		osCancelIo(handle, ovl) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: ovl)
		osGetPid then break 'u'
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the streaming stdin pipe reads four value operands (its two out-pointers are
		// still live operands); `osWriteFile` reads handle+buf+len+bytesOutPtr, its `resultId` a def skipped here.
		osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtr, saPtr, childReadPtr, parentWritePtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: namePtr, b: saPtr, c: childReadPtr, d: parentWritePtr)
		osWriteFile(_, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: len, d: bytesOutPtr)
		// The out-param ADDRESS is the one value operand the wall-clock read has, and it must be reported or
		// the allocator would free its register before the OS call writes through it — the same obligation
		// `osReadClock`'s two addresses carry. It defines nothing.
		osReadWallClock(ftPtr) then referenced.insert(ftPtr)
		// P1.7a slice 2b-v: the THROWING witness call reads exactly what `witnessCall` reads — the witness-table
		// address and its arguments — through the SAME helper. Both its results (`resultId`, `errorFlagId`) are
		// defs, exactly as `tryCall`'s two are.
		witnessTryCall(_, _, witnessAddr, _, _, _, args) then insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced, calleeReg: witnessAddr, args: args)
		// R4.1: the file OPEN reads its path BUFFER address; `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osFileOpenRead(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		// The close reads its HANDLE and defines nothing.
		osFileClose(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		// R4.2. Each reads its VALUE operands (an out-param ADDRESS is a live operand for the reason
		// `osReadWallClock`'s is: the allocator must not free its register before the OS writes through it);
		// every `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osReadFile(_, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) then insertUseQuad(referenced, a: handle, b: buf, c: len, d: bytesOutPtr)
		osFileOpenWrite(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osFileSize(_, handle, sizeOutPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: sizeOutPtr)
		osFileDelete(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osFileRename(_, oldPathPtr, newPathPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: oldPathPtr, b: newPathPtr)
		osFileFindFirst(_, pathPtr, findDataPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: pathPtr, b: findDataPtr)
		osFileFindClose(handle) then referenced.insert(handle)
		osFileFindNext(_, handle, findDataPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: findDataPtr)
		osDirCreate(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		osDirCurrentPath(_, bufLen, bufPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufLen, b: bufPtr)
		osFileAttributes(_, pathPtr) then referenced.insert(pathPtr)
		// W5. No operands: the `STD_INPUT_HANDLE` selector is a backend immediate, so this reads nothing at
		// all — `osCommandLine` exactly. Its `resultId` is a def and is skipped here.
		osStdinHandle then break 'u'
		// Both the buffer ADDRESS and the LENGTH are live value operands, for the reason `osDirCurrentPath`'s
		// pair are: the allocator must not free either register before the OS writes through the first.
		osExecutablePath(_, bufLen, bufPtr) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufLen, b: bufPtr)
		// W35: `osWriteStdout`'s arm one stream over, and it reads exactly what that one reads.
		osWriteStderr(_, bufId, lenId) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: bufId, b: lenId)
		// A promoted record's address reads NOTHING — its `slot` is a frame index, not a value, exactly as
		// `globalAddr`'s `label` is a name. Its `resultId` is a def.
		stackRecordAddr then break 'u'
		// O3: the segment open reads three pointers/counts, the map reads a handle and a size, and the tick
		// read has no operand at all.
		osOpenNamedSegment(_, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) then insertUseTriple(referenced, a: namePtr, b: bufPtr, c: bufLen)
		osMapNamedSegment(_, handle, size) then insertUsePair(referenced, a: handle, b: size)
		osTickCountMs then break 'u'
	end 'u'
end 'collectStdOpUses'

// ⭐ **WHAT AN OP DEFINES — the exact dual of `collectStdOpUses` above, and it lives beside it for that
// reason.** `nextFreeValueId` is the SHARED one: it began as `InsertRangeChecks`' private helper and
// `promoteStackRecords` needs the same answer, and a second "one past the highest id in this function"
// would be a second chance to hand out an id something already defines. `stdOpDefinedValue` below is
// still read only by `nextFreeValueId` — it is not exported, and it is here because the answer it gives
// belongs beside `collectStdOpUses`' rather than inside one pass that happens to have needed it first.
// The next unused ValueId in the function: one past the highest id any op DEFINES or any block-arg
// carries. Scanning defs (and phi results) suffices — dense SSA numbers every value once, so the
// maximum def id is the maximum id, and a use can only name an id that some def already introduced.
export function nextFreeValueId(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns ValueId
	var maxId = 0 as ValueId
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachArg'
			maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: arg.valueId)
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachBlock'
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		match stdOpDefinedValue(op) 'defines'
			none then break 'defines'
			defines(definedId) then maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: definedId)
			definesPair(first, second) then maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: maxValueId(first, other: second))
		end 'defines'
	end 'eachOp'
	return maxId + 1
end 'nextFreeValueId'

function maxValueId(a ValueId, other ValueId) returns ValueId
	return a if a >= other else other
end 'maxValueId'

// Whether `op` DEFINES a value, and WHICH — ONE exhaustive `StdOp` match answering both halves.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS TWO, AND WHAT KEPT THEM AGREEING WAS NOTHING** (P1.9 review). `stdOpDefinesValue`
// answered "does it define?" over a range-arm partition of the union and `stdOpDefinedIdOrZero`
// answered "which id?" over a destructured one — ~105 lines of parallel arms a reader had to diff by
// eye. A new `StdOp` variant is a compile error in BOTH, which is what the exhaustive match buys; but
// adding it as `true` in the first and `0` in the second COMPILES, and the consequence is not a
// diagnostic: `nextFreeValueId` under-counts, this pass mints a ValueId some op already defines, and
// two values collide in a function nothing about the guard being emitted names. A wrong answer one
// arm's worth of inattention away — the shape the duplication rule exists for.
//
// It also retires a SENTINEL: the id half returned `0` for a non-defining op and was safe only
// because every caller asked the boolean first — and ValueId 0 is a REAL id in this compiler (a
// zero-user-parameter generic method's layout-descriptor slot legitimately holds it, which is why
// `MaxonToStdLowering` carries a separate `hasImplicitLayout` bit rather than testing for 0). The
// union says "no value" in a way no ValueId can spell.
union DefinedValueLookup
	none
	defines(valueId ValueId)
	// ⭐⭐ **TWO DEFS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHY THIS ARM EXISTS — IT CLOSED A LATENT MISCOMPILE.**
	// `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` write a PRIMARY result AND an ERROR FLAG. Reported by the primary alone —
	// which is what this reader used to do, in as many words — `nextFreeValueId` can return an id that a
	// two-result op already defines, and the next pass to mint from it then defines a value some `tryCall`
	// also defines. Two live values share one id, in a function nothing about the minting site names, with
	// no diagnostic anywhere. `InsertRangeChecks` mints from exactly that number.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHICH OF THE TWO IS HIGHER IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE, AND MUST NOT BE.** One emission site spells
	// `errorFlagId = resultId + 1` (LowerMaxonToStd's builtin-conformance call); the others take both ids
	// from the parser's own mint, from a reserved pairing, or from a `try await` desugar, and nothing holds
	// them in an order. So this reports BOTH and the walk takes the maximum — which is right whichever way
	// round they came, where a rule about their order would be right only until the next producer.
	definesPair(first ValueId, second ValueId)
end 'DefinedValueLookup'

// The `none` set is the terminators / stores / effect ops that define nothing. Exhaustive over StdOp,
// matching `collectStdOpUses`'s discipline — a new variant is a compile error here rather than a
// silently-missed def.
function stdOpDefinedValue(op StdOp) returns DefinedValueLookup
	return match op 'r'
		const(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		binOp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		unaryOp(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		cmp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		binOpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		cmpImm(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		div(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		mod(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		condBranch to branch gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		ret gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		param(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		call(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		retVoid gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		globalAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		loadIndirect(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		storeIndirect gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		rdataAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.definesPair(resultId, second: errorFlagId)
		errorReturn to osExit gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osAllocPages(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W35: a stream write DEFINES the byte count the OS reports — it used to define nothing, back when
		// its one consumer was a void `print`.
		osWriteStdout(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		funcAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		callIndirect(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// P1.5 subprocess: only `osWaitHandle` (the `WaitForSingleObject` poll) defines a value; the other four
		// consume their `BOOL` via memory or ignore it (see StdDialect's ⭐ note).
		osWaitHandle(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osPanic to osCloseHandle gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// `osFreePages` (P1.5-B1a′) defines nothing; it sits past `osWaitHandle` in the union, so its own arm.
		osFreePages gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// The jump table defines no value — a terminator branches, it does not produce a result.
		switch gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// A witness call DEFINES its result (the dispatched method's return value), like `callIndirect`. The
		// throwing form defines that result AND an error flag, and reports BOTH — see `definesPair`.
		witnessCall(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.definesPair(resultId, second: errorFlagId)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the FIVE value-defining IOCP ops (iocp/event/thread handles, the read BOOL, the
		// last-error code) capture RAX; osPipeCreate/osLock*/osProcessSpawnInherit define nothing (see StdDialect).
		osPipeCreate gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osIocpCreate(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osEventCreate(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osThreadCreate(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osLockInit to osLockLeave gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osLastError(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osProcessSpawnInherit gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osCancelIo gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osGetPid(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: osWriteFile captures WriteFile's BOOL (a value); osPipeCreateOutbound defines nothing.
		osPipeCreateOutbound gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osWriteFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The wall-clock read defines nothing — its answer lands in the caller's out-param word.
		osReadWallClock gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.1: the file OPEN captures `CreateFileA`'s HANDLE out of RAX, so unlike the two reads above it
		// DOES define a value — the direct-return shape, not the out-param one.
		osFileOpenRead(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// The close defines NOTHING — `CloseHandle`'s BOOL is ignored, so there is no result to capture.
		osFileClose gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.2. Split exactly as R4.1's pair is: the five ops whose Win32 entry point answers in RAX — a
		// BOOL verdict or a search HANDLE — capture it; `osFileFindClose` discards `FindClose`'s BOOL for
		// the reason `osFileClose` discards `CloseHandle`'s. The 64-bit answers of `osReadFile`/`osFileSize`
		// land in the caller's out-param word, not in a result.
		osReadFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileOpenWrite(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileSize(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileDelete(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileRename(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileFindFirst(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileFindClose gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		// R4.3. All four directory primitives answer in RAX and capture it: `FindNextFileA`/
		// `CreateDirectoryA` a BOOL, `GetCurrentDirectoryA` a character count, `GetFileAttributesA` an
		// attribute mask. None has a void twin, so none takes `none`.
		osFileFindNext(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osDirCreate(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osDirCurrentPath(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osFileAttributes(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// A3n: the RENDERED panic reads a message pointer; in every other respect it is `osPanic`.
		osPanicValue gives DefinedValueLookup.none
		osCommandLine(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W5. BOTH DEFINE — `GetStdHandle` a HANDLE, `GetModuleFileNameA` a character count. Neither has a
		// void twin, so neither takes `none`, and answering `none` here would not fail to compile: it would
		// under-count `nextFreeValueId` and let this pass mint a live id (see this function's header).
		osStdinHandle(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osExecutablePath(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// W35: the stderr write defines the OS's byte count, exactly as its stdout twin above does.
		osWriteStderr(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// A promoted record's address is a def like `globalAddr`'s.
		stackRecordAddr(resultId, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		// O3: each of the three defines the OS answer it captures from RAX.
		osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, _, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osMapNamedSegment(resultId, _, _) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
		osTickCountMs(resultId) gives DefinedValueLookup.defines(resultId)
	end 'r'
end 'stdOpDefinedValue'

// Mark every call argument value as used, so a `const` fed ONLY to a call (`f(5)`) is not
// pruned as dead. A `param` has no uses (its result is a def), so it contributes nothing.
function insertUseArgs(referenced StdValueUseSet, args ValueIdArray)
	for arg in args 'each'
		referenced.insert(arg)
	end 'each'
end 'insertUseArgs'

function insertUsePair(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
end 'insertUsePair'

// The three-operand twin of `insertUsePair` — for the subprocess ops that read three value operands
// (`osProcessSpawn`'s cmdline + two out-struct pointers; `osWaitHandles`'s handle array + count + timeout).
function insertUseTriple(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
end 'insertUseTriple'

// The four-operand twin — for the P1.5 streaming ops that read four value operands (`osPipeCreateOutbound`'s
// name + SA + two out-pointers; `osWriteFile`'s handle + buf + len + bytesOut-pointer).
function insertUseQuad(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId, d ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
	referenced.insert(d)
end 'insertUseQuad'

// The five-operand twin — for the P1.5 IOCP ops that read five value operands (`osPipeCreate`'s name + SA + iocp +
// two out-pointers; `osReadOverlapped`'s handle + buf + cap + bytesOut-pointer + overlapped).
function insertUseQuint(referenced StdValueUseSet, a ValueId, b ValueId, c ValueId, d ValueId, e ValueId)
	referenced.insert(a)
	referenced.insert(b)
	referenced.insert(c)
	referenced.insert(d)
	referenced.insert(e)
end 'insertUseQuint'

// An indirect call reads its callee register AND every argument. The callee is a value operand (the
// code pointer), unlike a direct `call` whose callee is a NAME — so it must be inserted alongside the
// args or the value holding the function pointer would look dead and be pruned.
function insertUseCalleeAndArgs(referenced StdValueUseSet, calleeReg ValueId, args ValueIdArray)
	referenced.insert(calleeReg)
	insertUseArgs(referenced, args: args)
end 'insertUseCalleeAndArgs'

// `op` with every READ mapped through `subst` — a DENSE column, `subst[v]` being what `v` is
// rewritten to (and `v` itself where nothing changes). The mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: it
// rewrites exactly the operands that one reports, so the two cannot drift. DEFS are left alone
// (a def is not a use), and so are branch-edge args — those live on the predecessor's `IrBlock`,
// not on the op, so rewriting them is the caller's job. Exhaustive over StdOp with no bare
// `default`, for the same reason its twin is: a new variant must be a COMPILE ERROR here rather
// than a silently-missed rewrite at every call site.
//
// A COLUMN, NOT A (from, to) PAIR, and that is a performance contract rather than a convenience.
// The pair form could only rewrite ONE value per walk, so a caller with k substitutions to make
// walked the function k times — O(ops x k), and worse, it rebuilt every branch edge k times, which
// made its ALLOCATIONS quadratic too. `elimTrivialBlockArgs` had exactly that shape and it made
// the pass 54% of a large compile. The column form applies all k in a single walk.
module function remapStdOpUses(op StdOp, subst ValueIdArray) returns StdOp
	return match op 'r'
		const gives op
		binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.binOp(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.unaryOp(resultId, operand: remappedValue(operand, subst: subst), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) gives StdOp.cmp(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, operandType) gives StdOp.binOpImm(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), imm: imm, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
		cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) gives StdOp.cmpImm(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), imm: imm, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
		div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) gives StdOp.div(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), signedness: signedness)
		mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) gives StdOp.mod(resultId, lhs: remappedValue(lhs, subst: subst), rhs: remappedValue(rhs, subst: subst), signedness: signedness)
		condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) gives StdOp.condBranch(remappedValue(condId, subst: subst), thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId)
		branch gives op
		ret(retValId) gives StdOp.ret(remappedValue(retValId, subst: subst))
		param gives op
		call(resultId, callee, args) gives StdOp.call(resultId, callee: callee, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		retVoid gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s three arms: `globalAddr` reports no use,
		// so it rewrites nothing and is handed back unchanged.
		globalAddr gives op
		loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) gives StdOp.loadIndirect(resultId, addrId: remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) gives StdOp.storeIndirect(remappedValue(addrId, subst: subst), offset: offset, valueId: remappedValue(valueId, subst: subst), valueType: valueType)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the size is the one read, so it is the one rewrite.
		osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId) gives StdOp.osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId: remappedValue(sizeId, subst: subst))
		// The mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: both operands are reads, so both are rewritten; `resultId` is a
		// def and is carried through unremapped, exactly as every other defining system op's is.
		osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId, lenId) gives StdOp.osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId: remappedValue(bufId, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(lenId, subst: subst))
		// `rdataAddr` reports no use (its `label` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr` arms.
		rdataAddr gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `tryCall` rewrites its args (its two results are defs),
		// `errorReturn` rewrites both operands it reads, `osExit` rewrites its code operand.
		tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, callee, args) gives StdOp.tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, callee: callee, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) gives StdOp.errorReturn(remappedValue(retValId, subst: subst), errorFlagId: remappedValue(errorFlagId, subst: subst))
		osExit(codeId) gives StdOp.osExit(remappedValue(codeId, subst: subst))
		// `funcAddr` reports no use (its `funcName` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`/`funcAddr` arms.
		funcAddr gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `callIndirect` rewrites its callee register and its args
		// (its result is a def); `resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata carried through unchanged.
		callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg: remappedValue(calleeReg, subst: subst), resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// `osPanic` reads no value (its `label` is a name), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back
		// unchanged — the mirror of `funcAddr`/`rdataAddr`.
		osPanic gives op
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`: `osReadClock` rewrites both out-param addresses it reads,
		// `osSleepMs` rewrites its millisecond count. Both define nothing.
		osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) gives StdOp.osReadClock(remappedValue(ticksPtrId, subst: subst), freqPtrId: remappedValue(freqPtrId, subst: subst))
		osSleepMs(msId) gives StdOp.osSleepMs(remappedValue(msId, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s subprocess arms: rewrite exactly the operands they read;
		// `osWaitHandle`'s `resultId` is a def, so it passes through unmapped.
		osProcessSpawn(cmdlinePtr, startupPtr, procInfoPtr) gives StdOp.osProcessSpawn(remappedValue(cmdlinePtr, subst: subst), startupPtrId: remappedValue(startupPtr, subst: subst), procInfoPtrId: remappedValue(procInfoPtr, subst: subst))
		osProcessExitCode(handle, codePtr) gives StdOp.osProcessExitCode(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), codePtrId: remappedValue(codePtr, subst: subst))
		osWaitHandles(handleArrayPtr, count, timeoutMs) gives StdOp.osWaitHandles(remappedValue(handleArrayPtr, subst: subst), countId: remappedValue(count, subst: subst), timeoutMsId: remappedValue(timeoutMs, subst: subst))
		osCloseHandle(handle) gives StdOp.osCloseHandle(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osWaitHandle(resultId, handle, timeoutMs) gives StdOp.osWaitHandle(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), timeoutMsId: remappedValue(timeoutMs, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the region base is the one read, so it is the one rewrite.
		osFreePages(addr) gives StdOp.osFreePages(remappedValue(addr, subst: subst))
		osPanicValue(messagePtr) gives StdOp.osPanicValue(remappedValue(messagePtr, subst: subst))
		osCommandLine(resultId) gives StdOp.osCommandLine(resultId)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `switch` arm: only the biased index `subjectId` is a value
		// operand, so it is the one rewrite; the block-id table and default carry through verbatim. Binding
		// the middle-slot Array is safe — a trailing non-Array field (`defaultBlockId`) follows it, exactly
		// the condition that lets `call` bind its trailing args here.
		switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) gives StdOp.switch(remappedValue(subjectId, subst: subst), denseTargetBlockIds: denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId: defaultBlockId)
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `witnessCall` arm: rewrite the witness-table address and the
		// args (its result is a def; `methodIndex`/`resultType`/`argFloatMask` are metadata carried through).
		witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr: remappedValue(witnessAddr, subst: subst), methodIndex: methodIndex, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s P1.5 IOCP arms: rewrite exactly the value operands they read;
		// the result-only ops (`osIocpCreate`/`osEventCreate`/`osLastError`) rewrite nothing.
		osPipeCreate(namePtr, saPtr, iocp, readPtr, writePtr) gives StdOp.osPipeCreate(remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), saPtrId: remappedValue(saPtr, subst: subst), iocpId: remappedValue(iocp, subst: subst), readPtrId: remappedValue(readPtr, subst: subst), writePtrId: remappedValue(writePtr, subst: subst))
		osIocpCreate gives op
		osEventCreate gives op
		osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtr) gives StdOp.osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId: remappedValue(entryPtr, subst: subst))
		osLockInit(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockInit(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osLockEnter(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockEnter(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osLockLeave(lockPtr) gives StdOp.osLockLeave(remappedValue(lockPtr, subst: subst))
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, handle, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr, ovl) gives StdOp.osReadOverlapped(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), capId: remappedValue(cap, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst), ovlId: remappedValue(ovl, subst: subst))
		osLastError gives op
		osProcessSpawnInherit(cmdlinePtr, startupPtr, procInfoPtr) gives StdOp.osProcessSpawnInherit(remappedValue(cmdlinePtr, subst: subst), startupPtrId: remappedValue(startupPtr, subst: subst), procInfoPtrId: remappedValue(procInfoPtr, subst: subst))
		osCancelIo(handle, ovl) gives StdOp.osCancelIo(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), ovlId: remappedValue(ovl, subst: subst))
		osGetPid gives op
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: the streaming stdin pipe + write, rewriting exactly the value operands they read.
		osPipeCreateOutbound(namePtr, saPtr, childReadPtr, parentWritePtr) gives StdOp.osPipeCreateOutbound(remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), saPtrId: remappedValue(saPtr, subst: subst), childReadPtrId: remappedValue(childReadPtr, subst: subst), parentWritePtrId: remappedValue(parentWritePtr, subst: subst))
		osWriteFile(resultId, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) gives StdOp.osWriteFile(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s arm: the out-param address is the one read, so it is the
		// one rewrite. It defines nothing.
		osReadWallClock(ftPtr) gives StdOp.osReadWallClock(remappedValue(ftPtr, subst: subst))
		// The exact mirror of `collectStdOpUses`'s `witnessTryCall` arm: rewrite the witness-table address and the
		// args; both results are defs and every remaining field is metadata carried through.
		witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) gives StdOp.witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, witnessAddr: remappedValue(witnessAddr, subst: subst), methodIndex: methodIndex, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, args: remappedArgs(args, subst: subst))
		// R4.1: rewrite the path buffer; `resultId` is a def and is carried through unremapped, exactly as
		// every other defining system op's is.
		osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileClose(handle) gives StdOp.osFileClose(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		// R4.2: the exact mirrors of `collectStdOpUses`'s seven arms — every value operand it reports read is
		// rewritten here, and every `resultId` is a def carried through unremapped.
		osReadFile(resultId, handle, buf, len, bytesOutPtr) gives StdOp.osReadFile(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), bufId: remappedValue(buf, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(len, subst: subst), bytesOutPtrId: remappedValue(bytesOutPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileSize(resultId, handle, sizeOutPtr) gives StdOp.osFileSize(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), sizeOutPtrId: remappedValue(sizeOutPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtr, newPathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtrId: remappedValue(oldPathPtr, subst: subst), newPathPtrId: remappedValue(newPathPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtr, findDataPtr) gives StdOp.osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst), findDataPtrId: remappedValue(findDataPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileFindClose(handle) gives StdOp.osFileFindClose(remappedValue(handle, subst: subst))
		osFileFindNext(resultId, handle, findDataPtr) gives StdOp.osFileFindNext(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), findDataPtrId: remappedValue(findDataPtr, subst: subst))
		osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLen, bufPtr) gives StdOp.osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst))
		osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtr) gives StdOp.osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtrId: remappedValue(pathPtr, subst: subst))
		// W5. The exact mirrors of `collectStdOpUses`'s two arms: the handle read has no value operand to
		// rewrite, and the path fill rewrites exactly the pair it reads.
		osStdinHandle(resultId) gives StdOp.osStdinHandle(resultId)
		osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLen, bufPtr) gives StdOp.osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst))
		// W35: `osWriteStdout`'s arm one stream over.
		osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId, lenId) gives StdOp.osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId: remappedValue(bufId, subst: subst), lenId: remappedValue(lenId, subst: subst))
		// Reports no use (its `slot` is a frame index), so it rewrites nothing and is handed back unchanged —
		// the mirror of `globalAddr`/`rdataAddr`/`funcAddr`.
		stackRecordAddr gives op
		// O3: the two operand-carrying primitives remap theirs; the tick read has none, so it is handed back
		// unchanged like `stackRecordAddr` above.
		osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen) gives StdOp.osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId: remappedValue(namePtr, subst: subst), bufPtrId: remappedValue(bufPtr, subst: subst), bufLenId: remappedValue(bufLen, subst: subst))
		osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handle, size) gives StdOp.osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handleId: remappedValue(handle, subst: subst), sizeId: remappedValue(size, subst: subst))
		osTickCountMs gives op
	end 'r'
end 'remapStdOpUses'

// IDENTITY for any id outside the column. The column is sized by the BLOCK-ARG id space (the only
// values a phi-folding pass ever rewrites), and an operand above it — an op result, a parameter —
// is by definition not a block-arg and so is not being rewritten. Same discipline, and the same
// reason, as `pruneDeadBlockArgs.flagOf`.
module function remappedValue(v ValueId, subst ValueIdArray) returns ValueId
	return try subst.get(v) otherwise v
end 'remappedValue'

function remappedArgs(args ValueIdArray, subst ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
	var out = ValueIdArray.create()
	for a in args 'each'
		out.push(remappedValue(a, subst: subst))
	end 'each'
	return out
end 'remappedArgs'

// --- The sugar band contract ---
//
// v1 enforced "no sugar reaches the backend" with a TYPE: sugar ops existed in
// `StdOp` and simply had no `MirOp` counterpart, so the Std→MIR lowering could not
// represent them past the boundary. Collapsing the tier gives that up, and the
// replacement is deliberate rather than accidental — two independent guards:
//
//   1. Each sugar variant gets an explicit `panic("… must be desugared by
//      lowerToMachineForm")` arm in the backend's `match` — NOT a bare `default`,
//      which the project forbids precisely because it swallows new cases.
//   2. `assertNoSugarOps` below, a whole-module gate. It catches an op the
//      per-variant arms would miss — one reachable only through a `match` range arm,
//      or sitting in a block the backend's own walk happens not to visit.
//
// Both are vacuous at M1 (no sugar variants exist yet), so `lowerToMachineForm`
// itself is NOT written now. It arrives with the first sugar op (M6's `drop`/`free`,
// M14's descriptor/witness ops), as a Std→Std pass.
//
// The gate is run by `buildBackend` — the single TARGET-NEUTRAL backend entry — not
// inside `lowerStdToX64`. Sugar is a property of the Std tier, not of x64, so every
// future backend (arm64, wasm) inherits the check instead of having to remember to
// re-install it.
//
// It walks LIVE ops via `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, NOT the flat `module.ops` array:
// `lowerToMachineForm` is a block-rebuilding pass, so the sugar ops it desugars
// survive in `module.ops` as orphans. Scanning the flat array would panic on those
// dead entries and report a desugaring failure that never happened.
export function assertNoSugarOps(module StdModule)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("assertNoSugarOps: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			if op.rawValue.category == StdOpCategory.sugar 'isSugar'
				panic("Std→Target: a `sugar`-category StdOp survived into the backend in function '{String.from(func.name)}' — lowerToMachineForm did not run, or does not handle this op")
			end 'isSugar'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'assertNoSugarOps'
